{
  "format": "myo_sim.muscle_passive/5",
  "description": "Tendon slack lengths (absolute, m) and passive-force scales (fpmax) of the extrinsic hand muscles, fitted per Binder-Markey & Murray 2017 (Eqs. 2-3: least squares on passive joint moments plus (1/100) x sum of relative slack changes) with MuJoCo's passive force-length curve, MyoHand's muscle-tendon lengths and moment arms (scripts/fit_muscle_passive.py). MuJoCo's passive curve is far stiffer than the one Binder-Markey used and MyoHand's tendons are rigid, so each muscle also gets its own fpmax (passive force only; the active force is unchanged), with a mild penalty (joint_fit.fpmax_penalty) on |ln(fpmax / 1.3)|. reference_value_m / reference_value are the values the fit started from (myo_sim with fix/anatomical-muscle-parameters, i.e. the MoBL-ARMS / Saul et al. 2015 slack lengths, also Binder-Markey's initial values, and MuJoCo's default fpmax 1.3). Hard constraint (active_constraint): the active force-length factor of the finger and thumb flexors in a full fist (wrist neutral and flexed 30 deg) and of the extensors in full extension (wrist extended 30 deg) stays at least 0.5; on MuJoCo's curve this is a window on each slack length (tendon_slack_m.active_window_percent), and a slack length is shortened only as far as that window forces it. Index (FDS2, FDP2, EDC2, EIP): Knutson et al. 2000's extrinsic MCP moment over MCP 0..90 deg (MyoHand's MCP has no hyperextension) and wrist -60..60 deg. Other extrinsic finger, thumb and wrist muscles (joint_fit.muscles): no extrinsic-only data exist, so the target is the total passive moment of each joint_targets row and the loss is one-sided: a muscle moment beyond the total, or against its sign, costs joint_fit.excess_weight times a deficit, because joint springs can supply a deficit but only a cancelling element could remove an excess. Slack changes are bounded by joint_fit.max_change, fpmax by joint_fit.fpmax_bounds. Status 'transferred target' marks a muscle constrained by a curve measured on another digit. A release step (release_constraint) then refits only the fpmax of EDC3-5 and EDM, above the smallest value at which the digits open in the McFarland et al. 2023 tenodesis release trial. The model itself is myoarm_r_muscle.xml: scripts/fit_muscle_passive.py writes the lengthrange and fpmax of each muscle there (left arm mirrored).",
  "muscle_model_note": "The fitted slack lengths and fpmax are for MuJoCo's rigid-tendon muscle: its active and passive force-length curves, with the whole muscle-tendon excursion in the fibres. With compliant tendons the fit is redone: it fits the slack length plus a tendon or passive parameter (tendon strain at peak force, or a passive-force scale or strain) with each muscle at its fibre-tendon force equilibrium in every fitted posture, starting from the slack lengths here. fpmax, a scale on MuJoCo's passive curve, has no counterpart in common Hill-type formulations (e.g. De Groote et al. 2016) and does not carry over.",
  "sources": {
    "binder_markey2017": {
      "citation": "Binder-Markey BI, Murray WM. Incorporating the length-dependent passive-force generating muscle properties of the extrinsic finger muscles into a wrist and finger biomechanical musculoskeletal model. J Biomech 61:250-257, 2017.",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.jbiomech.2017.06.026",
      "access": "closed; method (Eqs. 1-8) and Table 2 verified from the PDF"
    },
    "knutson2000": {
      "citation": "Knutson JS, Kilgore KL, Mansour JM, Crago PE. Intrinsic and extrinsic contributions to the passive moment at the metacarpophalangeal joint. J Biomech 33(12):1675-1681, 2000.",
      "doi": "10.1016/S0021-9290(00)00159-7",
      "access": "closed; equations (1)-(4) and Table 1 verified from the PDF"
    },
    "formica2012": {
      "citation": "Formica D, Charles SK, Zollo L, Guglielmelli E, Hogan N, Krebs HI. The passive stiffness of the wrist and forearm. J Neurophysiol 108(4):1158-1166, 2012.",
      "doi": "10.1152/jn.01014.2011",
      "access": "open (PMC3424077); Table 2 and the posture (hand closed around the robot handle and bound shut, elbow flexed about 60 deg) verified from the paper"
    },
    "kamper2002": {
      "citation": "Kamper DG, Hornby TG, Rymer WZ. Extrinsic flexor muscles generate concurrent flexion of all three finger joints. J Biomech 35(12):1581-1589, 2002.",
      "doi": "10.1016/S0021-9290(02)00229-4",
      "access": "closed; Table 2 (tau_s, K, B) and Eq. (1) verified from the PDF"
    },
    "barry2018": {
      "citation": "Barry AJ, Murray WM, Kamper DG. Development of a dynamic index finger and thumb model to study impairment. J Biomech 77:206-210, 2018.",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.jbiomech.2018.06.017",
      "access": "paper verified from the PDF (Sec. 2.1: thumb MCP/IP passive set equal to index MCP/PIP; Domalain 2010 used for thumb CMC abduction); model file behind a simtk login"
    },
    "mcfarland2023": {
      "citation": "McFarland et al. A musculoskeletal model of the hand and wrist capable of simulating functional tasks. IEEE Trans Biomed Eng 70(5):1424-1435, 2023.",
      "doi": "10.1109/TBME.2022.3217722",
      "access": "tenodesis protocol as reimplemented in scripts/validate_hand_function.py (build/data/hand_function.json); cited only"
    }
  },
